    "question": {
      ":question/id": "68b2825f-3857-5c32-8ef8-b848aea295ca",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={3}^{2x+1}$ at the point where $x=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={3}^{u},\\,u=2x+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}={3}^{u}\\ln(3),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & ={3}^{u}\\ln(3)\\cdot (2) \\\\ & =(2)\\cdot {3}^{u}\\ln(3) \\\\ & =2\\cdot {3}^{2x+1}\\ln(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=0$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big|_{x=0} & =(2)\\cdot {3}^{2(0)+1}\\ln(3) \\\\ & =2\\cdot {3}^{1}\\ln(3) \\\\ & =6 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=0$ is $6 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}.$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=0$ is\n\n$$\ny_1={3}^{1}=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1&=m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(3)&=(6 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)})\\left(x-(0)\\right) \\\\ y&=6 x \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} + 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
